          "content": {
            "id": "p35-q14-root",
            "type": "question",
            "label": "14",
            "prompt": "Decide whether the inverse of $y=\\frac{2^x-2^{-x}}{2}$ is a function.\n\n(You will need to differentiate it to decide whether it is strictly increasing or decreasing).",
            "answerSpaceMm": 40,
            "answer": {
              "short": "Yes. $f'(x)=\\frac{\\ln 2\\,(2^x+2^{-x})}{2}>0$ for all real $x$, so $f$ is strictly increasing and its inverse is a function.",
              "worked": {
                "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
                "version": 1,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "id": "p35-q14-answer",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "align": "left",
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{2^x\\ln 2+2^{-x}\\ln 2}{2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{\\ln 2\\,(2^x+2^{-x})}{2}.\\end{align*}",
                        "display": true
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "p35-q14-answer-reason",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "align": "left",
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Since "
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "\\ln 2>0",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " and both exponential terms are positive, "
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "f'(x)>0",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": " for every real "
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "math",
                        "latex": "x",
                        "display": false
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": ". Thus the function is strictly increasing throughout its domain and is one-to-one. Its inverse is therefore a function."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              "provenance": {
                "short": "source",
                "worked": "authored"
              }
            }
          },
          "sourceReview": {
            "teachingContext": [
              {
                "pageNumber": 26,
                "taughtMethod": "A strictly increasing or strictly decreasing function is one-to-one and has an inverse function."
              },
              {
                "pageNumber": 27,
                "taughtMethod": "Relate the domain of an inverse to the range of the original function."
              }
            ],
